ngs happen here  and are then exported to the rest of the  country the tax revolt of proposition 13  a greater openness on social issues like  gay rights the digital revolution of  Silicon Valley all these things begin  here and move to the rest of the country  but if you read what was written about  California in the century after the gold  rush roughly 1850 to 1950 it's a much  different picture California is  portrayed not as the cutting edge of the  American experience but is totally  outside of the American experience its  distant geographically but it's also  different economically culturally  linguistically Carrie McWilliams a great  California writer perhaps the greatest  writer about California issues wrote a  book in 1949 assessing the California  experience and he concluded that  California was as he titled the book the  great exception California wasn't  typical of the American experience it  was the exception to the American  experience  so why this change why have people gone  from seeing California as being outside  of the American experience to being the  leading edge of the American experience  well I think that the turning point was  the era in which Pat was governor the  50s and the 60s  and I think that people began to think  about California differently because in  that time California typify door led the  way in three great waves or three great  phenomena of American history that then  moved to the rest of the country and  people began to see California as  setting the pace and in each of these  three things Pat is integral to those  stories and those experiences are  integral to his career the first is the  kind of thing for which we remember Pat  most vividly and that is post-war  optimism growth energy in the wake of  World War two the entire country was an  optimistic and growing place but nowhere  was that more true than in California in  1959 when Pat became governor the state  was growing by about 500,000 residents a  year that's about o